      <div>
        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Myriokephalon</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">17 September 1176</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Tzivritze Pass in the mountains west of Iconium (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>), west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>; the exact location is debated</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>, with Hungarian, Antiochene and Serbian contingents, estimated at 25,000 to 30,000 men by John Haldon and about 35,000 by John Birkenmeier; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a>, whose numbers cannot be estimated, though modern historians think such a state could field at most 10,000 to 15,000 men</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Byzantine army was ambushed in the pass and lost its siege train, and about half of the part engaged is thought to have become casualties. The campaign against Iconium was abandoned and a truce followed.</span></div>
        </div>
