    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Byzantine army was ambushed in the pass of Tzivritze, and the sultan, not the emperor, was the first to propose peace.</li><li>Estimates of the Byzantine army run from 25,000 to 30,000 men (Haldon) to about 35,000 (Birkenmeier), while no estimate of Seljuk numbers has been possible.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> won a notable victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> at Hyelion and Leimocheir the next year, but it was never again able to compete for dominance of the Anatolian interior.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> himself compared his defeat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a>, sending a message to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> ahead of his army that likened his fate to that of Romanos Diogenes.</p></aside>
    </section>

