    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1158–1161</span><span class="rf-text">A series of Byzantine campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> ends in a treaty favourable to the Empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1174</span><span class="rf-text">Nur ad-Din Zangi dies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> is free to destroy the Danishmend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> and expel his brother Shahinshah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1175</span><span class="rf-text">The peace collapses when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan</a> refuses to hand over the Danishmend territory he had conquered</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1176</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> I gathers a large army at Lopadion and marches towards Iconium</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1176</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> ambush the Byzantine army in the Tzivritze Pass</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1176</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan</a> sends the envoy Gabras to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> to negotiate a truce</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1177</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> at Hyelion and Leimocheir on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1179</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> concludes a probably advantageous peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-myriokephalon" data-v="8bbf2c8ac4" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Byzantine column enters the pass</b> The Byzantine divisions entered the pass in a long column, vanguard first and the rear division last. The Seljuks held the heights above it, perhaps not yet fully deployed.</li><li><b>The leading divisions clear the pass</b> The vanguard and main division went through with few casualties. Their infantry climbed the slopes and forced the Seljuks back to higher ground, while the divisions behind did not take this precaution.</li><li><b>The trap closes on the right wing and baggage</b> With the first two divisions out of the pass and the rear about to enter, the Turks attacked from the heights. The right wing broke and Baldwin was killed. The Turks then shot down the draught animals of the baggage and siege trains and choked the road.</li><li><b>The left wing is struck and a dust-storm falls</b> The left wing also took heavy casualties and John Kantakouzenos was killed. The remaining Byzantine troops panicked as the Turks began to attack the rear, a dust-storm came down, and Manuel sat down to await his fate.</li><li><b>Manuel forms up and leaves the pass</b> Roused by his officers, Manuel restored discipline and formed a defensive order. His troops pushed past the wrecked baggage and out of the pass to the van and main divisions, who had built a fortified camp. The rear division arrived later with few casualties.</li><li><b>The camp is attacked and counterattacks follow</b> The Seljuk mounted archers attacked the camp through the night and were repulsed. Next day the Turks circled the camp shooting arrows, and Manuel ordered two counterattacks, but there was no renewal of a general action.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos.jpg" alt="Manuel I Komnenos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_I_Comnenus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploader: Tokle, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between 1158 and 1161 a series of Byzantine campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> ended in a treaty favourable to the Empire, with the sultan recognising a form of subordination to the emperor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> then visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> treated him as both an honoured guest and an imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The peace was fragile. The Seljuks wanted to push from the arid central plateau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> into the more fertile coastal lands, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> wanted to recover the Anatolian territory lost since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> a century earlier.</p><p>During the long peace Manuel defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and imposed Byzantine control over all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from local Armenian dynasts and reduced the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to vassal status. The peace also allowed Kilij Arslan to eliminate internal rivals and strengthen his military resources. When Nur ad-Din Zangi, the strongest Muslim ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, died in 1174, his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> was more concerned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> than with the lands bordering the Empire. Kilij Arslan destroyed the Danishmend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and ejected his brother Shahinshah, Manuel's vassal, from his lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. Shahinshah and the Danishmend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> fled to Byzantium. In 1175 the peace fell apart when Kilij Arslan refused to hand over a considerable proportion of the territory he had taken from the Danishmends, as the treaty obliged him to do.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Béla III of Hungary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III of Hungary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A9pes_kr%C3%B3nika_-_122.oldal_-_III._B%C3%A9la_kir%C3%A1ly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Márk Kálti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Byzantine army was of exceptional size. John Haldon estimates it at 25,000 to 30,000 men, and John Birkenmeier at around 35,000, a figure derived from a supply train of 3,000 wagons, enough to support 30,000 to 40,000 men. Birkenmeier believes it held 25,000 Byzantine troops, the remainder being Hungarians sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a>'s kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III of Hungary</a> and tributary forces from the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. It was divided into divisions that entered the pass in order: a vanguard largely of infantry; the main division of eastern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagma-military">tagmata</a>; the right wing, largely Antiochenes and other Westerners, under Baldwin of Antioch, Manuel's brother-in-law; the baggage and siege trains; the left wing under Theodore Mavrozomes and John Kantakouzenos; the emperor and his picked troops; and the rear division under the experienced general Andronikos Kontostephanos.</p><p>No estimate of Seljuk numbers for the battle has been possible. Modern historians think the successor states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, could field at most 10,000 to 15,000 men. The army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> in 1097 has been put at 6,000 to 8,000. The Seljuk force had two parts: the askars of the sultan and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, full-time soldiers, often mamluks, who fought as armoured medium to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> with bow and lance; and irregular Turkoman tribesmen, light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> who served for plunder, ransom or payment and were numerous but unreliable.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> gathered his army at Lopadion and marched towards the Seljuk border by way of Laodicea, Chonae, Lampe, Celaenae, Choma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan</a> tried to negotiate, but Manuel, convinced of his superiority, rejected a new peace. He sent part of the army under Andronikos Vatatzes towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasia</a> while his larger force marched towards Iconium. Both routes ran through heavily wooded country, and the army heading for Amasia was destroyed in an ambush. The Turks also destroyed crops and poisoned water supplies, and harassed the main army to drive it towards the pass of Tzivritze near the fortress of Myriokephalon. Manuel chose to force the pass, though the plain of Philomelion was nearby. Lack of forage and water and an outbreak of dysentery may have influenced him.</p><p>The vanguard and main division went through the pass with few casualties, and their infantry climbed the slopes to dislodge the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. The following divisions did not take this precaution, kept no closed ranks and did not use their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> well. As the rear was about to enter, the Turks closed the trap. Their attack fell especially heavily on the right wing, which lost cohesion, and Baldwin was killed. The Turks then attacked the baggage and siege trains, shooting down the draught animals and choking the road. John Kantakouzenos was slain fighting alone. A blinding dust-storm added to the confusion, and Manuel reputedly sat down passively until his officers roused him. He formed a defensive order, pushed past the wreck of the baggage and out of the pass, and joined the van and main divisions, which had built a fortified camp. Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> were repulsed through the night. The next day the Turks circled the camp firing arrows, Manuel ordered two counterattacks, and there was no general action.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Myriokephalon&#8221; cites where it covers Battle:</p><ul><li>John Haldon, The Byzantine Wars (Tempus, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert.jpg" alt="Battle of Manzikert" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:131_Bataille_de_Malazgirt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rohan Master, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Both sides suffered casualties, though their extent is difficult to quantify. Modern historians have postulated that about half of the Byzantine army was engaged and around half of those became casualties. Most importantly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a>'s siege equipment had been captured and destroyed, and without means of attacking Iconium the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> could not continue the campaign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan</a> sent an envoy named Gabras with a Nisaean warhorse and a sword to negotiate a truce. The army was to be allowed to retreat unmolested if Manuel destroyed his forts and evacuated the garrisons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> and Sublaeum.</p><p>Despite the sultan's protestations of good faith, Turkoman tribesmen, over whom he probably had little control, harassed the retreat. This and an earlier failure by the sultan to keep a treaty signed in 1162 gave Manuel an excuse not to observe the new terms in full. He demolished the fortifications of the less important Sublaeum but left Dorylaeum intact. He sent a message to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> comparing his fate to that of Romanos Diogenes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a>, yet also boasted of the treaties made beneath his own banner. A possible reason for Kilij Arslan's reluctance to renew the battle is that many of his irregulars were more interested in securing their plunder than in continuing the fight.</p><p>The next year the Byzantines won a notable victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> at Hyelion and Leimocheir on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a>, where a Seljuk army blundered into an ambush laid by the general John Komnenos Vatatzes. Manuel continued to meet the Seljuks in smaller battles with some success and concluded a probably advantageous peace with Kilij Arslan in 1179.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Myriokephalon&#8221; cites where it covers Outcome, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Michael Angold, The Byzantine Empire 1025–1204: A Political History (Longman, 1984)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates.jpg" alt="Niketas Choniates" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niketas_Choniates.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Buchhändler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a> records that near the pass stood the ruins of a fortress called Myriokephalon, which gave the battle its name. He also says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> considered abandoning his troops and was shamed into staying by an anonymous soldier and by Kontostephanos. A modern historian thinks this hyperbole, since flight would have put Manuel in greater danger than staying with his army. Manuel's own message to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, comparing his defeat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> while boasting of the treaties, is also reported.</p><p>Much remains uncertain. The exact location of the battle is debated among researchers. Seljuk numbers cannot be estimated, and the Byzantine figures rest on a supply train of 3,000 wagons and on estimates by Haldon and Birkenmeier. Casualties are hard to quantify. Modern historians have judged that about half the army was engaged and about half of those fell. It was reported that the Byzantine dead were scalped and mutilated, so that, as the report has it, the circumcised could not be told from the uncircumcised and the victory could be disputed. The sultan's opening of peace talks leads one historian to conclude that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan</a>, though negotiating from strength, did not think his forces able to destroy the Byzantine army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel</a> compared his defeat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a>, and the battle has been called pivotal in the same way. Following it, the balance between the two powers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> gradually began to shift, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> was unable to compete for dominance of the Anatolian interior. One account holds that it was the final, unsuccessful Byzantine effort to recover the interior of Anatolia, and that after Manuel's death the empire drifted into anarchy and never again mounted a major offensive in the east. The same account holds that the Anatolian plateau was lost to the empire for ever.</p><p>Other views weigh it differently. Myriokephalon did not materially affect the capabilities of the Byzantine army, as the victory at Hyelion and Leimocheir the next year underlines, and it had more of a psychological than a military impact, proving that the Empire could not destroy Seljuk power in central Anatolia. One view is that Manuel was distracted by adventures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> instead of dealing with the Turks. It has also been argued that his preoccupation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> was aimed at securing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and Anatolia, and that the campaign of 1176 was meant to put right the neglect of nearer matters.</p><p>Assessments of Manuel's generalship differ. He made serious tactical errors, failing to scout the route ahead effectively and ignoring the advice of his senior officers. In his defence, he organised the army in self-reliant divisions, each able to act as a small independent army, and it has been argued that this let the greater part of it survive the ambush. The failure of the infantry vanguard to sweep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> from the heights, and of the wing commanders to deploy as well as the leading divisions, is counted a major cause of the defeat.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 17 September 1176 the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> ambushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>'s Byzantine army in the Tzivritze Pass west of Iconium. Manuel's siege train was lost and the campaign ended, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> never again contested the Anatolian interior.</em></p>

